  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about which modality, encoder, profile setting, or fusion strategy contributes most to validation accuracy. The entire design space is unexplored. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the simplest possible configuration—single modality, single profile, linear PCA encoder with no aggregation or fusion. It establishes a clean performance floor for the RNA signal alone, with no confounding from image features, learned encoders, multi-profile aggregation, or fusion. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: Under a limited budget, the first experiment should be fast, interpretable, and set a reference against which every subsequent design choice can be measured. PCA is computationally trivial, singleprofile avoids aggregation complexity, and rna_only isolates one modality. This baseline lets me attribute any future gain unambiguously to adding image features, a learned encoder, multiprofile aggregation, or multimodal fusion. It also minimizes risk of a failed or ambiguous run, preserving budget for more informative follow-up comparisons (e.g., config_010 for image-only, or config_016 for the first multimodal test)."
}

